    v=2^j     mean X  as a multiple of log2   |prod| <= 2^k * exp(-k*mean)
        4   0.346574               0.500000                2^{-k * 0.5000}
        8   0.519860               0.750000                2^{-k * 0.7500}
       16   0.606504               0.875000                2^{-k * 0.8750}
       32   0.649825               0.937500                2^{-k * 0.9375}
       64   0.671486               0.968750                2^{-k * 0.9688}
      128   0.682317               0.984375                2^{-k * 0.9844}
      256   0.687732               0.992188                2^{-k * 0.9922}
  the minimum over all v is at v = 4: (1/2)log 2, i.e. 2^(-k/2) = (1/sqrt2)^k = 0.707107^k
  every other v (odd, or divisible by 4 with an odd part > 1) gives the full
  log 2, i.e. 2^(-k).  The obstruction is at the powers of two and nowhere
  else -- which is prop:twopower, reached from an exact evaluation instead of
  from the group-theoretic characterisation.

======================================================================================
4.  the surrogate ceilings: what a bounded surrogate costs before any
    equidistribution input is invoked
======================================================================================
  mean over the circle of         measured       exact            factor lost
  X(t) itself                     0.693147    0.693147     log 2       1.000x
  (1/2) sin^2(pi t)               0.250000    0.250000       1/4       2.773x
  (pi^2/2) ||t||^2                0.411234    0.411234   pi^2/24       1.686x
  both surrogates are valid pointwise:
    X(t) >= (1/2)sin^2       violations        0   equality only at t = 0
    X(t) >= (pi^2/2)||t||^2  violations        0   equality only at t = 0
  they are valid and they are lossy: X has a logarithmic singularity at the
  half-integers and the surrogates do not, and that singularity carries the
  difference between 1/4 and log 2.

